## A General MIDI synthesiser: Standard MIDI Files in, rendered sound out
## (`doc/agentic/audio-design.md`, decisions D9 and D10). The synthesiser plays
## a SoundFont bundled with it, at the audio output's rate, `rate`.
##
## Both directions travel on the interface's io-channel (xbufs): the client
## submits packets naming buffers of the channel's data pages, the synthesiser
## handles them in the order they were put and hands each one back. A packet's
## value word says what it is; its low byte is the command:
##
## - 1, song: its records hold bytes of a Standard MIDI File, which the
##   synthesiser appends to the song it is collecting. Bit 8 starts a new song
##   (the bytes collected so far are dropped), bit 9 ends it: the song replaces
##   what played and plays from its start, looping when bit 10 is set too. A
##   song too large for the data pages travels in several packets.
## - 2, render: its records are empty buffers, which come back holding the next
##   frames of the music, signed 16-bit stereo, left then right, little-endian,
##   each record cut to the whole frames it holds (silence when nothing plays).
## - 3, stop: the song ends, its notes fade.
## - 4, pause and 5, resume: while paused, rendered frames are silent and the
##   song holds its place.
## - 6, volume: bits 8 to 15 are the music volume, 0 to 127 (127 at start).
##
## A command without data still names one record (an empty one will do). Bits
## 16 and up are the client's own, handed back unchanged with the packet. A
## packet with a record outside the data pages, or of an unknown command,
## comes back unhandled with its records emptied.
##
## The synthesiser handles packets during `service` only, on the caller's
## Thread: a client puts its packets, calls `service`, and finds them all back
## when it returns. Prototype: edited in place.
local interface midi_synth_v1
{
    ## The io-channel: a control page and sixteen data pages, room for four
    ## periods of rendered frames and the rest for song bytes.
    channel 16;

    ## Every function receives the badge of the key it was called through: the
    ## synthesiser tells its clients apart by it.
    badged;

    ## Another client holds the synthesiser.
    exception busy {}

    ## The caller does not hold the synthesiser.
    exception not_open {}

    ## The rate the synthesiser renders at in millihertz: 44 091 710, the audio
    ## driver's (100 MHz over 2268).
    rate()
        returns (card32 millihertz);

    ## Take the synthesiser for the caller: nothing plays, the volume is full.
    open()
        raises (busy);

    ## Give the synthesiser back; what played stops at once.
    close()
        raises (not_open);

    ## Handle every packet the caller put on its io-channel, in order, and hand
    ## each one back. Returns the number of packets handled.
    service()
        returns (card32 handled)
        raises (not_open);

    ## Whether a song plays: 1 from the end of its last song packet until it
    ## ends or is stopped (a looping song plays until stopped), else 0.
    playing()
        returns (card32 playing)
        raises (not_open);

    ## The most voices that sounded at once (of 32) in the frames rendered
    ## since the caller last asked: how hard the song works the synthesiser.
    voices()
        returns (card32 peak)
        raises (not_open);
}
